<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!--
      ⚠ THERE IS NO android.permission.INTERNET HERE, AND THAT IS THE POINT.

      Sacred Rule 1: the phone is the only place the ledger lives. Leaving INTERNET out
      means Android *enforces* that, rather than the app promising it. No library, no
      future session, and no mistake can send a transaction anywhere, because the OS
      will refuse the socket.

      Do not add it. The one thing that would ever justify it is the optional Gemini
      insight call at v1.1 task 14 — and that is a deliberate, separate decision,
      governed by Sacred Rule 12.

      SMS permissions arrive at PLAN task 6, not before.
    -->

    <!--
      READ_SMS: the inbox sweep and the first-run backfill (PLAN task 5). This is what
      lets months of history appear the day the app is installed, and what catches any
      message the live receiver missed.

      Sacred Rule 2 is a promise the app keeps, not one Android enforces: this permission
      grants access to every message on the phone, and Sika filters to the MoMo sender in
      the query itself so nothing else is ever loaded. That asymmetry is exactly what the
      Reddit commenter meant by "a big privacy surface" — worth knowing, and the reason
      this app is sideloaded to one phone rather than published.

      RECEIVE_SMS and the broadcast receiver arrive at task 6, not here.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.READ_SMS" />

    <!--
      RECEIVE_SMS: the live route (PLAN task 6). This is what lets a transaction be recorded
      the moment MoMo texts, without the app being opened.

      A separate permission from READ_SMS despite sharing a group — one reads history, the
      other wakes the app on arrival. Both are requested at runtime.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECEIVE_SMS" />

    <!--
      POST_NOTIFICATIONS: the cash-out prompt (PLAN task 12) and the monthly report (task 14).

      A runtime permission since Android 13. ⚠ Without it `notify()` neither throws nor
      posts — the notification simply never appears — so the app checks it explicitly rather
      than discovering the silence later. See CashOutPrompt.canPost.

      Refusing it costs the prompt, not the ledger: a cash-out is still recorded and can
      still be labelled by hand from the transaction sheet.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />

    <!--
      RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED: the end-of-day nudge.

      Android discards every scheduled alarm when the phone restarts. Without this the
      nudge would work perfectly until the first reboot and then stop for good - silently,
      because nothing appears to break. This is the only reason it is here.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED" />

    <!--
      required="true", not the required="false" lint suggests.

      Lint asks for this tag so an app does not accidentally exclude tablets and ChromeOS,
      and its default suggestion is `required="false"` meaning "runs fine without a modem".
      That would be false here: Sika's entire input is SMS, so on a device with no
      telephony it has nothing to do. Declaring the truth is better than declaring the
      thing that makes the warning go away.

      No practical cost — this app is sideloaded to one Pixel and is never published, so
      store filtering does not apply.
    -->
    <uses-feature
        android:name="android.hardware.telephony"
        android:required="true" />

    <!--
      Backup is off twice over, and both halves are deliberate. Android's automatic cloud
      backup would copy the ledger to Google Drive, and device transfer would send it to a
      new phone — the OS doing on the app's behalf exactly what the missing INTERNET
      permission exists to prevent. `allowBackup="false"` is the old switch;
      `dataExtractionRules` is the Android 12+ one, and it excludes every domain.
      See res/xml/data_extraction_rules.xml.
    -->
    <application
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/data_extraction_rules"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/Theme.Sika">

        <!-- No android:label here: it would repeat the application's, which lint flags
             as RedundantLabel. The launcher uses the application label. -->
        <!--
          singleTop: tapping a cash-out prompt's body must bring the existing app forward
          and hand it the row id, not stack a second copy of Sika on top of the first.
          onNewIntent is what receives it in that case.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:launchMode="singleTop"
            android:exported="true">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

        <!--
          The live route. Declared here rather than registered in code, which is what lets
          Android start the app for a message when nothing of Sika is running.

          android:permission restricts who may deliver this broadcast to holders of
          BROADCAST_SMS — which in practice is the system alone. Without it, any app on the
          phone could fabricate a transaction and Sika would record it as real money.

          exported="true" is required: the sender is the OS, not this app.

          Note on implicit broadcasts: Android 8 stopped most of them waking manifest-declared
          receivers, and this would not work at all if SMS_RECEIVED were not on the
          exemption list. It is, which is the only reason the design works.
        -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".sms.SmsReceiver"
            android:exported="true"
            android:permission="android.permission.BROADCAST_SMS">
            <intent-filter android:priority="999">
                <action android:name="android.provider.Telephony.SMS_RECEIVED" />
            </intent-filter>
        </receiver>

        <!--
          Answers to the cash-out prompt.

          exported="false" and no intent-filter: the only thing that can reach this is the
          PendingIntent handed to the system when the notification was posted. Exporting it
          would let any app on the phone relabel rows in the ledger without a trace.
        -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".notify.CashOutReplyReceiver"
            android:exported="false" />

        <!--
          What the missing money was, typed into the balance-gap alert.

          exported="false" for the same reason as the one above: the only thing that may
          reach it is the PendingIntent this app handed to the system.
        -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".notify.GapReplyReceiver"
            android:exported="false" />

        <!--
          The end-of-day nudge, and its re-scheduling after a restart.

          exported="false" but WITH an intent-filter, which looks contradictory and is not:
          BOOT_COMPLETED is delivered by the system regardless of the exported flag, while
          everything else on the phone stays locked out. The alarm itself arrives through a
          PendingIntent, which needs no export either.
        -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".notify.DailyNudgeReceiver"
            android:exported="false">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.BOOT_COMPLETED" />
            </intent-filter>
        </receiver>
    </application>

</manifest>
